// say.fk — a voice mid-program, through a door that is always open.
//
import form/form-stdlib/core.fk;
//
// WHAT SAY IS. fkwu's `print` is the value printer: each operand rendered by its kind,
// one space between operands, a newline after, and the call answers nothing. `say` is
// the byte-exact voice beside it: it writes the text as given, with no separator and no
// newline added, and answers the byte count so a caller can tell that it happened.
//
// THE DOOR. stdout is a file, and `write_file_text` writes files:
//
//     (write_file_text "/dev/stdout" "hello\n")
//
// `say` is a name no native owns, so it replaces nothing present — the same shape as
// `fol-bp`.
//
// PROVENANCE (2026-07-26, measured). `say` was written when a call to `print` recovered
// to nothing under axiom-5 and simply did not happen on fkwu. Counted that day, with
// comments and string literals stripped, `print` had 751 real call sites in 104 cells.
// The capability was never missing there; only the name was. fkwu now carries `print`
// natively, and `say` stays for the places that want bytes exactly as written.
//
// The glass speaks through it: form/form-stdlib/form-glass-live.bml.
// Proof: pending (no band reads this cell in this tree).
section [form.bml] {
    // ── the door, named ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    def say-stdout-path() = "/dev/stdout";
    // say — write text as it is, no newline added. Answers the byte count, so a
    // caller can tell that it happened.
    def say(s) = write_file_text(say-stdout-path(), s);
    // say-line — the common case.
    def say-line(s) = say((s + "\n"));
    // say-to — the same act aimed somewhere durable. The band uses this, because a
    // band that proved itself by writing to stdout would be proving it by being read.
    def say-to(path, s) = write_file_text(path, s);
    def say-line-to(path, s) = say-to(path, (s + "\n"));
    // say-count — a label and a number, the shape most diagnostics want.
    // NOTE the trap (measured 2026-07-26): (int_to_str 10.5) answers "" on fkwu.
    // Hand this integers. A fraction goes quiet here, which is how it hid the first time.
    def say-count(label, n) = say-line((label + (" " + n.str())));
    0;
}
